<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">THE OPERATOR’S DESK</span><h1>Good questions.<br><em>Clearer decisions.</em></h1><p class="lede">Practical starting points for a product conversation, a topology review or your next platform evaluation.</p></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">EVALUATION PLAYBOOK</span><h2>Bring the right information.</h2><div class="cards"><article><span>01 /</span><h3>Describe your edge</h3><p>Access handoffs, VLAN/QinQ, CPE types, PPPoE/IPoE mix, MTU, subscriber identity and upstream connectivity.</p></article><article><span>02 /</span><h3>Describe the workload</h3><p>Busiest-hour bandwidth, packet-size mix, subscribers, simultaneous flows, new-flow rate and routing scale.</p></article><article><span>03 /</span><h3>Describe the operation</h3><p>RADIUS, billing, service profiles, mapping retention, staff roles, maintenance windows and incident escalation.</p></article><article><span>04 /</span><h3>Define recovery</h3><p>Acceptable interruption, session continuity, configuration recovery, backup needs and site-loss expectations.</p></article><article><span>05 /</span><h3>Define acceptance</h3><p>A representative test topology, enabled features, thresholds, evidence capture and ownership of results.</p></article><article><span>06 /</span><h3>Plan the return path</h3><p>Source configuration, address ownership, routing, subscriber reconnection and accounting reconciliation.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">FREQUENTLY ASKED</span><h2>Straight to the useful part.</h2><div class="faqs"><details open><summary>Is LiquidNAT an internet service provider?</summary><p>LiquidNAT is an ISP network software platform. The customer operates the infrastructure and subscriber network; the product business supplies software and related services.</p></details><details><summary>Do we have to replace the entire network?</summary><p>Start with standalone CGNAT, a combined subscriber edge or the management and identity workflows you want to improve. Choose scope around your topology.</p></details><details><summary>How should we compare performance?</summary><p>Ask for a defined hardware/software profile and repeatable tests with the intended features enabled. Include packet rate, flow churn, logging, latency and recovery headroom.</p></details><details><summary>Can we keep our billing or RADIUS platform?</summary><p>Bring the current APIs, RADIUS attributes and lifecycle requirements to a technical review. Integration depends on the exact products, versions and workflows.</p></details><details><summary>Where does migration start?</summary><p>Inventory the service model, map the target configuration, validate representative journeys and agree a staged transition with a return path.</p></details><details><summary>Is a browser demonstration a throughput test?</summary><p>A browser simulation explains the interface and workflows. Packet processing, capacity and recovery require technical tests on the applicable system.</p></details><details><summary>How do we discuss pricing?</summary><p>Use the contact page. Share the intended deployment, capacity requirements, support expectations and migration scope so the conversation has the right context.</p></details></div></section>

<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">GO DEEPER</span><h2>From first look<br>to technical review.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>Addressing choices</h3><p>CGNAT capacity, historical lookup and dedicated addresses.</p><a class="text-link" href="cgnat.php">CGNAT, direct and static IPv4 →</a></article><article><h3>Deployment models</h3><p>Standalone translation, combined edge and regional gateways.</p><a class="text-link" href="deployment.php">Define the system boundary →</a></article><article><h3>Migration playbook</h3><p>Map service intent, test a cohort and rehearse the return path.</p><a class="text-link" href="migration.php">Plan the transition →</a></article><article><h3>Sizing & acceptance</h3><p>Include flows, packets, churn, enabled services and recovery.</p><a class="text-link" href="performance.php">Compare the combined workload →</a></article></div></section><?php require __DIR__ . '/includes/footer.php'; ?>
